xnu 0271: read the reports while a finger is down, not only when the line says so

The digitizer is not slow; the reader was. With the part sending at its 240 Hz
default, the report thread delivered 10-25 reports a second while a finger was
dragging (ring239: `poll 120/s ev 120/s touch 10-25/s`), because it only read
when the interrupt line was still low at the moment of the look, and the part
refreshes that line long before the next look.

The vendor's driver reads on every look while a finger is down and only uses the
line to know when nobody is touching. This does the same: `was[]` is the previous
report, so a finger that is down forces the read, and the line remains the gate
that keeps an untouched screen from costing anything.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_touch.c
@@ -1600,7 +1600,23 @@
 		 * this vsync, not every sample the part made.
 		 */
 		for (passes = 0; passes < 4; passes++) {
-			if (!mondrian_touch_poll &&
+			bool down = false;
+
+			/*
+			 * The line is low while a report waits, but only until the part
+			 * refreshes it: looked at 120 times a second it delivered 10-25
+			 * reports, and a drag then moves in steps of 40-100 ms whatever
+			 * the compositor does (measured, ring239). The vendor's own
+			 * driver reads on every look while a finger is down and keeps
+			 * looking between reports; do the same, and keep the line as the
+			 * idle gate so a screen nobody touches costs nothing.
+			 */
+			for (i = 0; i < MONDRIAN_TOUCH_FINGERS; i++) {
+				if (was[i]) {
+					down = true;
+				}
+			}
+			if (!mondrian_touch_poll && !down &&
 			    (mondrian_tlmm_read(mondrian_touch_irq_page, MONDRIAN_TLMM_IN_OUT) & 1) != 0) {
 				break;
 			}
